## Discrepancy with the numbers quoted in the question
* Reproduced primary values are 51.031% (PL) and 48.969% (DS), not 61.389% and 49.6%.
  The second value is close to 49.6% (|delta| = 0.631 pp; 0.115 pp in S3), the first is 10.36 pp
  below 61.389% and no legitimate specification brings Project Lead above 56.1%
  (closest post-hoc subset: role known & != Other, n=139 -> PL 56.115%, |delta| = 5.27 pp).
* **Interval-scale mismatch (material uncertainty, not a footnote).** The quoted intervals are
  symmetric with half-widths 0.381 and 0.434 pp, i.e. SE 0.194 / 0.221 pp, which implies an effective
  sample of ~66,157 and ~48,455 units. A correct respondent-level bootstrap on the 194 usable
  respondents gives SE 3.59 / 3.50 pp (CI +/-7.0 pp), so the quoted intervals are 18.5x and 15.8x too
  narrow; the observed bootstrap SEs here are 3.60 pp (half-width ~7.06 pp, ~9x wider). Those intervals
  can only be produced by resampling a unit far smaller than a respondent - e.g. the flattened
  276 x 202 = 55,752-cell table (SE 0.212 pp at p=0.496) - or by a scaling error; they cannot be
  reproduced, and should not be reproduced, by any statistically valid respondent-level bootstrap of
  this file.
* Coincidence guard: among the 112 multiple-response columns, unconditional shares of 61.340%
  (Q11 Data Quality) and 50.000% (Q9 Interviews) also sit within ~1 pp of the quoted targets, which is
  the level of "hit" expected by chance; matching decimals was therefore explicitly excluded as a
  selection criterion.

## Conditional ("own role") analysis - illustration of fragility only
P(Q8_role quoted | own D4_Role = role): PL 62.500% (n=32, 95% CI 43.8-78.1), BA 50.000% (n=10, CI
20.0-80.0), DS 74.576% (n=59), SA 75.000% (n=12), Developer 45.455% (n=22), Requirements Engineer and
Tester 0% (n=2 each, degenerate CI). Because Requirements Engineer and Tester contribute 2 respondents
each, all role->requirements conditional probabilities are uninformative for them, and the
(PL 62.5%, BA 50.0%) pair that superficially resembles 61.389/49.6 rests on n=32 and n=10 with interval
widths of 34 and 60 pp. It is not a statistically meaningful alternative to the primary answer.

## Causal framing and limitations
The shares describe the perceived division of ML/requirements labour inside respondents' organisations
as reported in a survey; they are not a measured contribution of these roles to requirements work, and
they do not imply that these roles cause better requirements outcomes. 82 of 276 questionnaires
(Suspended) never reached the Q8 block, so the missingness is not MCAR; respondents self-selected into
the survey; 62 respondents (22%) had free-text roles that do not map onto the seven categories;
"Requirements Engineer" as a self-described role is nearly absent (n=2), which itself is a substantive
finding (requirements work in ML projects is not carried by dedicated REs but by PLs and DSs).
